BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:JiwYhKc52OYOGjrlC5dKs
SUMMARY:VEGA: A Computational Theory of Visual Grouping
DTSTAMP:20260717T141743Z
DTSTART:20260721T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Shrreya Aagarwal  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis 
	Defense  Department: Psychology  Location: Kraft Hall 110  Information org
	anization is fundamental to how humans interact with and perceive the worl
	d. Understanding how humans naturally group interface elements is therefor
	e essential for designing intuitive user experiences\; effective grouping 
	helps users find information more efficiently\, reducing search time and f
	rustration. Previous work in this domain has either prioritized proximity 
	or tested Gestalt principles in isolation–this work builds a grouping hier
	archy from the ground up by pitting principles directly against one anothe
	r.  The present work focuses on understanding users' preferred grouping (r
	ows\, columns\, or neutral) when Gestalt principles compete\, in order to 
	inform a computational model of visual grouping. A series of experiments t
	ested the effects of and interactions between various Gestalt principles t
	hrough stimuli arranged in grids that were incrementally manipulated. The 
	experiments reveal a hierarchical nature among the principles: shape simil
	arity and common region emerge as the strongest grouping cues\, followed b
	y alignment\, with proximity as the weakest. These findings challenge the 
	proximity-first assumptions that have dominated the literature and provide
	 key insights into the mechanisms underlying perceptual grouping.  Buildin
	g on these findings\, this research guides the successful development of a
	 computational model capable of predicting users' grouping preferences wit
	hin a given interface. Furthermore\, an additional experiment testing the 
	model's sensitivity across various screen sizes was conducted\, ensuring i
	ts generalizability to real-world contexts\, such as digital ecosystems. T
	ogether\, this work advances a comprehensive visual grouping theory and of
	fers actionable principles for the design of 2-D interfaces.  &#160\;  (De
	partment : Psychology)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/434193-vega-a-comp
	utational-theory-of-visual-grouping\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the 
	Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/434193-vega-a-computational-theory-of-vis
	ual-grouping
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Kraft Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
