BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:_ZVkQMTHzMZeSJiv_1P54
SUMMARY:Strange Orientations: Forrest Bess and the Texas Gulf Coast
DTSTAMP:20260717T141743Z
DTSTART:20260723T140000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Corey Stout  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defen
	se  Department: Art History  In this dissertation\, I explore the signific
	ance and impact of the Gulf Coast on the work of Texas artist Forrest Bess
	 (1911-1977). Bess\, represented by Betty Parsons Gallery in the 1950s and
	 1960s\, moved to the coast to explore visions he had been having since ch
	ildhood. Although art historians have acknowledged the imagery of the coas
	tal landscape in his paintings\, scholarship on Bess has largely centered 
	on his visions and their symbolic meanings. This emphasis has obscured the
	 formative role of the coastal environment\, and I argue that the coast wa
	s not simply the setting for his exploration of these visions but was a ma
	terial and conceptual context that shaped his practice. Drawing on his art
	works\, photographs\, correspondence\, and archival materials\, I demonstr
	ate how his practice emerged through a reciprocal relationship between the
	 body and the coastal environment to produce a queer way of inhabiting spa
	ce that dissolves the boundaries between the two. I first examine how Bess
	 used modernist abstraction to produce a visual language that folded the b
	ody and coastal landscape into one another to explore queer embodiment and
	 subjectivity. I then consider how the material and spatial conditions of 
	the Gulf Coast expanded Bess's practice beyond the canvas to explore archi
	tecture\, photography\, labor\, and everyday life as sites of artistic pro
	duction. Finally\, I show that Bess engaged with petroleum and the oil ind
	ustry along the Texas Gulf Coast as a material framework for understanding
	 the intersection of queerness and madness. By repositioning Bess within t
	he context of the Texas Gulf Coast\, I expand our understanding of his art
	istic practice beyond painting\, while also demonstrating how Bess anticip
	ated later developments of performance\, conceptual\, and environmental ar
	t.  &#160\;  https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/2066552633?pwd=KzNsbTRwM0FQQ
	WQrWFFUMXdUMjJRZz09  (Department : Art History)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu
	/live/events/434296-strange-orientations-forrest-bess-and-the-texas\n\nFro
	m Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/434296-strange-orientations-forrest
	-bess-and-the-texas
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:See Zoom Link
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
